## Overview

This is stack layer **1/6** for [#68332 — Aspire Dashboard Native AOT support](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/issues/68332), bounded to the live PR diff `66e8a029..9ca33ec5` (77 files, 14 commits). It makes Blazor’s serialization boundaries metadata-first: framework contracts are source-generated, application contracts compose ahead of compatibility reflection, and converter-owned JS types remain converter-backed. The cross-cutting constraint is **host isolation**: application resolvers are snapshotted per service provider/host and never mutate process-static serializer options; later stack layers build on this JSON foundation for JS dispatch, binding, component metadata, framework providers, and the final Native AOT proof.

## Design

The application-facing JSON surface is deliberately narrow and experimental: one context property and one registration method. It does not expose component, binding, or JS-invokable descriptors owned by later layers.

```csharp
// src/Components/Web/src/Metadata/RazorComponentsMetadataContext.cs
// Applications expose an STJ resolver without giving framework consumers direct access
// to JsonSerializerContext instances or to unrelated component metadata.
[Experimental("ASPNETCORE9004", UrlFormat = "https://aka.ms/aspnet/analyzer/{0}")]
public abstract class RazorComponentsMetadataContext
{
    protected RazorComponentsMetadataContext()
    {
    }

    public abstract IJsonTypeInfoResolver? JsonTypeInfoResolver { get; }
}

// src/Components/Web/src/Metadata/ComponentMetadataServiceCollectionExtensions.cs
// Each registration contributes to options used to construct an immutable provider snapshot.
public static IServiceCollection AddComponentMetadata<TContext>(this IServiceCollection services)
    where TContext : RazorComponentsMetadataContext, new()
{
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(services);

    services.AddComponentJsonMetadata();
    var context = new TContext();
    if (context.JsonTypeInfoResolver is { } resolver)
    {
        services.Configure<ComponentJsonMetadataOptions>(options => options.Resolvers.Add(resolver));
    }
    services.AddSingleton<RazorComponentsMetadataContext>(context);

    return services;
}
```

Protected browser storage adds typed writes and an optional typed serializer while retaining the shipped object overloads, existing constructors, and JSON fallback behavior.

```csharp
// src/Components/Server/src/ProtectedBrowserStorage/ProtectedBrowserStorage.cs
// Existing object overloads remain. The generic overload roots the closed TValue service lookup.
public ValueTask SetAsync<TValue>(string key, TValue value)
    => SetAsync(CreatePurposeFromKey(key), key, value);

public ValueTask SetAsync<TValue>(string purpose, string key, TValue value)
{
    ArgumentException.ThrowIfNullOrEmpty(purpose);
    ArgumentException.ThrowIfNullOrEmpty(key);

    return SetProtectedJsonAsync(key, Protect(purpose, value));
}

// src/Components/Server/src/ProtectedBrowserStorage/ProtectedBrowserStorageSerializer.cs
[Experimental("ASPNETCORE9004", UrlFormat = "https://aka.ms/aspnet/analyzer/{0}")]
public abstract class ProtectedBrowserStorageSerializer<T>
{
    public abstract string Serialize(T value);
    public abstract T Deserialize(string data);
}
```

Resolver ordering is intentional and consistent:

| Boundary | Resolver order |
|---|---|
| Fixed framework DTOs | generated framework context → reflection when enabled |
| Marker/persistence/application payloads | generated framework context → registered application resolver(s) → reflection when enabled |
| Web/JS interop | fixed Web context → fixed JS context → converter-backed types → application resolver(s) → reflection when enabled |
| Protected storage | application resolver(s) → reflection when enabled; custom `ProtectedBrowserStorageSerializer<T>` wins before JSON |

The rejected alternative was process-static mutation of persistence or marker resolver chains. Instead, serializer/deserializer instances, `ComponentStatePersistenceManager`, renderers, and the WebAssembly host own their options snapshots. Therefore a provider already built is immutable; only providers built after another `AddComponentMetadata<TContext>()` call observe the new contracts.

## Implementation

**Framework-owned contracts.** The same generated-contract pattern covers four equivalence classes; one representative is shown, with the remaining deltas listed rather than repeated:

```csharp
// src/Components/Server/src/CircuitJavaScriptInitializationMiddleware.cs
// IList<string> is a closed framework payload, so the endpoint works even when STJ reflection is disabled.
public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)
{
    await context.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(
        _initializers,
        JavaScriptInitializersJsonContext.Default.IListString);
}

[JsonSerializable(typeof(IList<string>))]
private sealed partial class JavaScriptInitializersJsonContext : JsonSerializerContext;
```

- **Static framework data:** import maps, resource collections, JavaScript initializer manifests/responses, and prerender application-state dictionaries use generated `JsonTypeInfo` overloads.
- **Closed value families:** TempData generates its supported primitive, nullable, collection, dictionary, object-array, and `Dictionary<string, JsonElement>` shapes; generic-interface reflection remains available only when `JsonSerializer.IsReflectionEnabledByDefault`.
- **Circuit/persistence DTOs:** circuit root-operation batches, persisted marker dictionaries, persistent-service registrations, framework persistent primitives, and antiforgery state have generated contracts.
- **Marker/render-fragment DTOs:** Server and WebAssembly envelopes, component parameter definitions, and serialized render-tree nodes are generated; application parameter values deliberately continue into the application/reflection portion of the chain.

**Protocol options remain authoritative.** Generated metadata is resolved through the protocol-owned options rather than serializing directly with a context property. This preserves camel-case field names and null omission while avoiding reflection.

```csharp
// src/Shared/Components/ServerComponentSerializationSettings.cs
// Example wire shape remains {"type":"webassembly",...}, never {"Type":"webassembly",...}.
internal static JsonSerializerOptions CreateOptions(IJsonTypeInfoResolver? applicationResolver)
{
    var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
    {
        PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.CamelCase,
        PropertyNameCaseInsensitive = true,
        DefaultIgnoreCondition = JsonIgnoreCondition.WhenWritingNull
    };

    options.TypeInfoResolverChain.Add(ServerComponentJsonContext.Default);
    if (applicationResolver is not null)
    {
        options.TypeInfoResolverChain.Add(applicationResolver);
    }
    if (JsonSerializer.IsReflectionEnabledByDefault)
    {
        options.TypeInfoResolverChain.Add(CreateReflectionResolver());
    }

    return options;
}

// src/Components/Endpoints/src/Rendering/EndpointHtmlRenderer.Streaming.cs
var markerTypeInfo = ServerComponentSerializationSettings.JsonSerializationOptions
    .GetTypeInfo(typeof(ComponentMarker));
var serializedStartRecord = JsonSerializer.Serialize(marker, markerTypeInfo);

var endMarkerTypeInfo = ServerComponentSerializationSettings.JsonSerializationOptions
    .GetTypeInfo(typeof(ComponentEndMarker));
var serializedEndRecord = JsonSerializer.Serialize(endMarker, endMarkerTypeInfo);
```

The same options-owned contract rule applies to cached render fragments and marker serializers/deserializers. Raw marker tests assert lowercase `type`/`prerenderId`, absence of PascalCase names, and omission of null-only fields on both start and end comments.

**Provider-scoped application composition.** Registration is additive, but resolution snapshots the list once when the provider creates the singleton resolver.

```csharp
// src/Components/Web/src/Metadata/ComponentJsonMetadataResolver.cs
internal sealed class ComponentJsonMetadataResolver : IComponentJsonMetadataResolver
{
    public ComponentJsonMetadataResolver(IOptions<ComponentJsonMetadataOptions> options)
    {
        var resolvers = options.Value.Resolvers;
        JsonTypeInfoResolver = resolvers.Count switch
        {
            0 => null,
            1 => resolvers[0],
            _ => System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonTypeInfoResolver.Combine([.. resolvers]),
        };
    }

    public IJsonTypeInfoResolver? JsonTypeInfoResolver { get; }
}

// src/Components/Components/src/PersistentState/PersistentStateSerializationOptions.cs
// Every manager gets fresh options: framework first, its provider's app contracts next, reflection last.
public static JsonSerializerOptions Create(IJsonTypeInfoResolver? applicationResolver)
{
    var options = new JsonSerializerOptions(JsonSerializerOptionsProvider.Options);
    options.TypeInfoResolverChain.Clear();
    options.TypeInfoResolverChain.Add(PersistentStateJsonContext.Default);

    if (applicationResolver is not null)
    {
        options.TypeInfoResolverChain.Add(applicationResolver);
    }
    if (JsonSerializer.IsReflectionEnabledByDefault)
    {
        options.TypeInfoResolverChain.Add(CreateReflectionResolver());
    }

    return options;
}
```

Consumers fall into three identical ownership groups: persistence (`ComponentStatePersistenceManager`/`PersistentComponentState`), Server/Endpoints markers and Session (serializer/deserializer instances), and protected storage (DI-owned options). Directly constructed legacy instances retain shared compatibility options; DI-created instances snapshot the current provider.

**JS payloads.** `JSRuntime` now installs reflection explicitly only when available and serializes outbound arguments through the existing `object[]` type-info contract. Web renderers place fixed contracts and converter-backed references ahead of application contracts.

```csharp
// src/Components/Web/src/WebRenderer.cs
// Fixed wire types are first; converter-owned object/stream references do not need reflected members;
// app payloads follow; JSRuntime's compatibility reflection resolver remains last.
var resolvers = jsonOptions.TypeInfoResolverChain;
PlaceResolverAt(resolvers, WebRendererSerializerContext.Default, 0);
PlaceResolverAt(resolvers, WebJSInteropSerializerContext.Default, 1);
PlaceResolverAt(resolvers, ConverterBackedTypeInfoResolver.Instance, 2);
if (serviceProvider.GetService<IComponentJsonMetadataResolver>()?.JsonTypeInfoResolver is { } applicationResolver)
{
    PlaceResolverAt(resolvers, applicationResolver, 3);
}

// src/JSInterop/Microsoft.JSInterop/src/JSRuntime.cs
var argsJson = args is not null && args.Length != 0
    ? JsonSerializer.Serialize(args, JsonSerializerOptions.GetTypeInfo(typeof(object[])))
    : "[]";
```

The equivalent inbound deltas are explicit type-info overloads for custom events and JS component parameters; `EventArgs.Empty` needs no contract. Navigation payloads send the Server renderer ID as its integer wire representation.

**WebAssembly host timing and order.** Initial prerender parameter deserialization is deferred until after service-provider creation, so it can use that host's resolver snapshot. Root-update operations use the same host-owned context. Registered marker-backed roots are then inserted before manual mappings, retaining historical startup precedence and preserving order within both groups.

```csharp
// src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly/src/Services/WebAssemblyHostSerializationContext.cs
public WebAssemblyHostSerializationContext(RootTypeCache rootComponentCache, IServiceProvider services)
{
    _rootComponentCache = rootComponentCache;
    var resolver = ComponentJsonMetadata.GetApplicationResolver(services);
    ComponentOptions = WebAssemblyComponentSerializationSettings.CreateOptions(resolver);
    JSInteropOptions = DefaultWebAssemblyJSRuntime.Instance.CreateHostJsonSerializerOptions(resolver);
    _parameterDeserializer = new WebAssemblyComponentParameterDeserializer(
        new ComponentParametersTypeCache(),
        ComponentOptions);
}

// src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly/src/Hosting/WebAssemblyHostBuilder.cs
var registeredMappings = new RootComponentMapping[registeredComponents.Length];
for (var i = 0; i < registeredComponents.Length; i++)
{
    var registeredComponent = registeredComponents[i];
    var componentType = _rootComponentCache!.GetRootType(
        registeredComponent.Assembly!,
        registeredComponent.TypeName!)
        ?? throw new InvalidOperationException("Registered root component type was not found.");

    var parameters = serializationContext.DeserializeComponentParameters(registeredComponent);
    registeredMappings[i] = new RootComponentMapping(
        componentType,
        registeredComponent.PrerenderId!,
        parameters.Parameters);
}

for (var i = 0; i < registeredMappings.Length; i++)
{
    RootComponents.Insert(i, registeredMappings[i]);
}
```

**Protected storage dispatch.** The generic path first looks for a closed typed serializer; otherwise it resolves generated JSON metadata from the provider-owned options. Protection and browser I/O are unchanged.

```csharp
// src/Components/Server/src/ProtectedBrowserStorage/ProtectedBrowserStorage.cs
private string Protect<TValue>(string purpose, TValue value)
{
    var serializer = _services?.GetService<ProtectedBrowserStorageSerializer<TValue>>();
    var data = serializer is null
        ? JsonSerializer.Serialize(
            value,
            _serializerOptions.GetTypeInfo(value?.GetType() ?? typeof(TValue)))
        : serializer.Serialize(value);
    var protector = GetOrCreateCachedProtector(purpose);

    return protector.Protect(data);
}

private TValue Unprotect<TValue>(string purpose, string protectedJson)
{
    var protector = GetOrCreateCachedProtector(purpose);
    var data = protector.Unprotect(protectedJson);
    var serializer = _services?.GetService<ProtectedBrowserStorageSerializer<TValue>>();

    return serializer is null
        ? (TValue)JsonSerializer.Deserialize(data, _serializerOptions.GetTypeInfo(typeof(TValue)))!
        : serializer.Deserialize(data);
}
```

## Outcome

| Equivalence class | Validation/proof |
|---|---|
| Fixed framework contracts and reflection-last fallback | Reflection-disabled remote tests; persistent-state/TempData/initializer coverage; LinkabilityChecker build succeeded with 0 warnings/errors |
| Marker and render-fragment protocol | Full Endpoints suite **813/0**; focused marker/cache tests **75/0**; raw start/end JSON assertions; representative Server interactive E2E passed |
| Provider composition and isolation | Tests compose multiple contexts and prove an existing provider cannot observe later registrations; Web suite **316/0**, Server suite **405/0** |
| Web/JS payloads | Explicit outbound/inbound type-info coverage, converter-backed reference coverage, and focused JSInterop serialization tests |
| WebAssembly host ownership and ordering | WebAssembly suite **55/0**; two registered + two manual mapping order test; targeted `ClientRenderingMultpleComponentsTest.CanRenderMultipleRootComponents` passed in Debug JIT |
| Protected storage | Typed serializer, runtime-type fallback, direct-construction compatibility, failure behavior, and API baseline coverage |
| Cross-project integration | Dependency-aware Components E2ETest Debug and Release builds succeeded with 0 warnings/errors; shared nullable contexts compile when linked into nullable-disabled projects |

Restack verification against current `main`: all **14/14** commits are patch-identical (`range-diff` `=` and matching stable patch IDs), with no dropped or repaired PR patches. Post-restack validation passed Components **36/0**, Web **9/0**, Endpoints **45/0**, Server **51/0**, WebAssembly **5/0**, JSInterop **56/0**, plus the dependency-aware Components E2ETest compile gate with 0 warnings/errors.

This PR remains independently reviewable against `main`; subsequent stack layers depend on its application JSON registration and host-isolated serializer composition.
